    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 passing).\n\n## What I did\n\n**The rule:** A new owner's `membershipLevel` is capped at one above the current maximum level among the other members of their household (owners sharing the same deterministic `householdId`, derived from lastName + postcode). With no other household member, the uncapped level stands.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a transient `membershipLevel` field (mirrors the existing `householdSize`/`possibleDuplicate` transient, response-only derived fields).\n\n2. **`MembershipLevels.java`** \u2014 added `capped(owner, householdMembers)`: `min(of(owner), maxLevelOfOtherMembers + 1)`, or the uncapped `of(owner)` when there are no other members.\n\n3. **`AssignOwnerMembershipLevel.java`** (new step) \u2014 finds the other household members via the repository, aligns their `householdSize` so each is scored on the same basis a GET would report, and stores the capped level on the owner.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `membershipLevel` now reads the assigned transient, falling back to `MembershipLevels.of(owner)` when unset (so list endpoints that skip the step are unchanged).\n\n5. **POST and GET `{ownerId}` YAML pipelines** \u2014 inserted the `membershipLevel` step right after `householdSize` (the level depends on household size) in both, so the create response and later GET agree.\n\n**One consequential change worth flagging:** the provided test creates two same-household owners *without* `sharesHousehold` and expects both to succeed. The existing `ensureUniqueIdentity` step (a prior checkpoint's rule) rejected the second member with 409, which is incompatible with a cap that presupposes members coexist. I removed that step from the POST pipeline \u2014 the new policy allows joining a household and caps the level instead of rejecting. I left the `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` class and its escalation handler in place (now unused, no startup impact) rather than deleting them. If retaining the household-duplicate rejection is actually required, let me know and I'll reconcile the two rules differently.",
